WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.847 --> 00:00:04.136
There will often be times that you'll
need to query data from a table in your

2
00:00:04.136 --> 00:00:04.665
database.

3
00:00:04.665 --> 00:00:08.608
For example, you might need to retrieve
a list of names from a table, or

4
00:00:08.608 --> 00:00:11.640
return a set of results
from a math calculation.

5
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.517
You can perform these actions
using the select statement, and

6
00:00:14.517 --> 00:00:16.800
that's what I'll demonstrate now.

7
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.727
So over the next few minutes, you'll learn
how to utilize the SQL SELECT statement to

8
00:00:21.727 --> 00:00:25.901
query data from a table in a database and
perform other SQL SELECT tasks,

9
00:00:25.901 --> 00:00:31.340
such as math calculations, date and
time queries and concatenation functions.

10
00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.069
To get started, let's explore
the syntax of a SQL statement.

11
00:00:35.069 --> 00:00:38.707
A basic SQL SELECT statement is
written as the SELECT keyword,

12
00:00:38.707 --> 00:00:42.342
the name of the column that contains
the data you need to query,

13
00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:46.546
then the FROM keyword and finally
the name of the table you want to query.

14
00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:51.504
For example, if I want to query data about
the names of players from a soccer club

15
00:00:51.504 --> 00:00:55.722
database, I could use the following
syntax, the SELECT keyword,

16
00:00:55.722 --> 00:00:59.000
the player name, column and
the FROM keyword.

17
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.557
And finally the name of
the table which is player table.

18
00:01:02.557 --> 00:01:07.275
And although it's not mandatory a semi
colon is often added to mark the end of

19
00:01:07.275 --> 00:01:08.393
a SQL statement.

20
00:01:08.393 --> 00:01:11.510
Let's take a closer look
at how the statement works.

21
00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:16.129
As an example, I'll extract information
from a table called players held in

22
00:01:16.129 --> 00:01:17.630
a soccer club database.

23
00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:22.753
This table records details about
players in the soccer club like ID,

24
00:01:22.753 --> 00:01:24.885
name, age and skill level.

25
00:01:24.885 --> 00:01:29.191
I can use the SQL SELECT statement on
this table to obtain information on

26
00:01:29.191 --> 00:01:30.568
the club's players.

27
00:01:30.568 --> 00:01:35.310
The expected outcome of this select query
is that it will return results set that

28
00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.015
displays all player
names held in the table.

29
00:01:38.015 --> 00:01:43.930
I can write the statement as
SELECT name FROM players.

30
00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:46.970
The SELECT command is
used to retrieve data,

31
00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:51.850
names the column that stores the player's
name in the database, FROM is

32
00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.979
a keyword that identifies the source
table, and players is the table name.

33
00:01:56.979 --> 00:01:59.016
I then run the statement.

34
00:01:59.016 --> 00:02:03.558
The query returns a table column that
lists the player's names from the players

35
00:02:03.558 --> 00:02:05.611
table with each name on its own row.

36
00:02:05.611 --> 00:02:10.960
So in the example you just explored I
retrieved data from one column in a table.

37
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.340
But what if I wanted to retrieve
data from multiple columns?

38
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:17.820
Maybe I need to retrieve the name and
skill level of each player.

39
00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:23.892
I can obtain this information using
a SQL SELECT statement written as SELECT,

40
00:02:23.892 --> 00:02:26.471
name, level, FROM, players.

41
00:02:26.471 --> 00:02:28.950
I had a comma between name and level, So

42
00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:32.341
that SQL understands they
are two separate columns.

43
00:02:32.341 --> 00:02:35.278
I run the query and
it returns the data from the name and

44
00:02:35.278 --> 00:02:37.980
level columns in the players table.

45
00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.162
I could also use a SELECT statement
to retrieve all data from all

46
00:02:41.162 --> 00:02:43.510
columns in the players table.

47
00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:45.950
There are two methods I
could use to achieve this.

48
00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:50.112
The first method is to list all column
names in a standard select statement as

49
00:02:50.112 --> 00:02:50.707
follows.

50
00:02:50.707 --> 00:02:56.962
Select ID, name, age,
level, FROM, players.

51
00:02:56.962 --> 00:03:00.070
Once again each column
is separated by a comma.

52
00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:04.720
I then run the query and get all the
requested information and table format.

53
00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.210
The second method is to use
an asterisk as shorthand.

54
00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:11.616
So instead of typing out all column names,

55
00:03:11.616 --> 00:03:16.732
I just type select asterisk from players,
then I run the query.

56
00:03:16.732 --> 00:03:20.195
It returns all the information
available in all table columns,

57
00:03:20.195 --> 00:03:21.871
just like in the first method.

58
00:03:21.871 --> 00:03:25.849
You're now familiar with how to use
the select statement in my SQL.

59
00:03:25.849 --> 00:03:28.747
So, next time you need to
query data in your database,

60
00:03:28.747 --> 00:03:31.461
you've now got different
methods to choose from.